      "worked_solution": "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis only at at $x=1.$ \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has point of inflection at $x=1.$",
